// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
= Motivation
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Phase 1 established the MILP formulation and demonstrated optimality on the
5-node toy example. The formulation is exact, but its complexity profile is
problematic: the unique-slot constraints introduce $O(n^2)$ binary variables
$z_(i j)$, and the branch-and-bound search space grows *exponentially* with $n$.

Real BTreeFy applications typically deploy trees in the range $n = 20$–$200$
nodes. If the MILP solver cannot terminate within a practical time limit for
those sizes, an alternative approach — a heuristic — is needed. Phase 2 makes
this claim *empirical and reproducible* by measuring solver wall-clock time
across a range of $n$ values, documenting the exact point where CBC transitions
from tractable to intractable. This is the scientific motivation for Phase 3.

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
= Experimental Design
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

== Instance Generation

Random BT instances are generated by `random_bt.py` using a top-down
construction:

#algo(title: "Random BT construction (random_bt.py)")[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  #bullet[Start with a single root node (control node).]
  #bullet[Maintain a pool of *expandable* control nodes.]
  #bullet[At each step: pick a node from the pool; attach 2–4 children to it.]
  #bullet[New children join the pool only while unplaced nodes remain.]
  #bullet[Remaining budget assigned to root if pool empties prematurely.]
  #bullet[Reindex all nodes in DFS pre-order (matching the real parser output).]
  #bullet[Derive LCRS fields (parent/child/sibling) from the resulting tree.]
]

This ensures every generated graph is structurally valid: control nodes have
at least 2 children, leaves have no children, and all LCRS invariants hold.

== Protocol

#card(title: "Experiment parameters", color: accent)[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  #grid(columns: (1fr, 1fr), gutter: 14pt,
    [
      - *n values:* {5, 8, 10, 12, 15, 20}
      - *Instances per n:* 5 (seeds 0–4)
      - *Total runs:* 30
    ],
    [
      - *Time limit:* 300 s (5 min) per instance
      - *Solver:* PuLP 2.x / CBC backend
      - *Hardware:* local workstation (single thread)
    ]
  )
]

Per run, the following metrics are recorded:

- `solve_time` — wall-clock seconds until CBC returns (or times out)
- `milp_cost` — best MinLA objective value found
- `status` — `Optimal` / `Feasible` (timed out with incumbent) / `Timeout`
- `binary_vars` — $n(n-1)$, the count of $z_(i j)$ binary variables

== Complexity Analysis

The binary variable count $n(n-1)$ is the driver of intractability:

#card(title: "Binary variable count vs. n", color: warn)[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  #grid(columns: (auto, auto, auto, auto, auto, auto), gutter: 8pt,
    [*n*], [5], [8], [10], [12], [15],
    [*z vars*], [20], [56], [90], [132], [210],
    [*n*], [20], [25], [30], [50], [100],
    [*z vars*], [380], [600], [870], [2450], [9900],
  )
  The $O(n^2)$ growth means that doubling $n$ roughly *quadruples* the number
  of binary variables. CBC's branch-and-bound explores an exponential number of
  nodes in this binary space.
]

