// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
= Discussion
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

== Why Does CBC Fail Here?

Branch-and-bound on Mixed-Integer Programs has worst-case complexity
exponential in the number of binary variables. The $n(n-1)$ ordering
variables $z_(i j)$ create a search space of $2^{n(n-1)}$ candidate
combinations. Even with LP relaxations, cutting planes, and heuristic
branching, CBC cannot prune enough of this space to terminate within 5 minutes
once $n >= 15$.

Notably, the formulation is correct and complete — given infinite time, CBC
would find the global optimum for any $n$. The wall is purely computational,
not mathematical. This is precisely the NP-Hardness of MinLA manifesting
in practice. #ref-cite("Garey & Johnson, 1979")

== Why Not Tighten the MILP?

Several standard techniques can push the intractability boundary further (to
$n approx 20$–$25$): valid inequalities (e.g. symmetry-breaking constraints),
problem-specific cuts, or warm-starting CBC from a good heuristic solution.
These are deliberate scope-out decisions: the research objective is to
*characterise* the wall, not to maximise the reach of the exact solver.
The heuristic in Phase 3 must work well for $n = 20$–$200$, a range where
MILP is never competitive regardless of formulation tightening.

